{1}-{c}_{2}=9 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=15\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=5$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0d346a2-b012-5c0d-b429-60020e32ca6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129584 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7{c}_{2}=-7\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+3\\cdot 1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130288 #edb/ref 1061 587180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c26cbdb9-e4ae-57e4-8ad0-478213ccd227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130289 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130289 #edb/ref 1061 587181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0ac3561-63c3-5604-a2d7-f38c8ca2d824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130290 #edb/ref 1061 587184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "89c5622e-b252-5578-bb6d-ccdb5821b61d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130291 #edb/ref 1061 587187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fe5a7b9-5aff-56d9-8bcf-d38aed54ee82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130360 #edb/ref 1064 "An **initial value problem (IVP)** for a homogeneous linear system has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf{x}_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the given initial state at $t=t_0.$ In other words, in an IVP we seek the solution of $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ that passes through the point $\\mathbf{x}_0$ at $t=t_0.$\n\nTo solve an IVP, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Find the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\n- Substitute $t=t_0$ into the general solution and use the initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=c_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nc_1\\mathbf{x}_1(t_0)+c_2\\mathbf{x}_2(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\n- Solve the resulting linear system for $c_1$ and $c_2,$ then substitute those values back into $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\n\nThe definition (and the procedure) above generalizes directly to the $n\\times n$ case.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples of solving an IVP." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130360 #edb/ref 1065 3651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130360 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "92ec67c9-079c-5825-825b-08cca4b2cf25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130360 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299933 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)=-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299933 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299933 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "006c6611-e018-504a-a01c-32aef354674d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300173 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 